<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Salih ibn Tarif</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">8th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Salih ibn Tarif was the second leader of the Barghawata, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> confederation on the Atlantic coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>. Around 744, after his father Tarif had joined the Berber revolt against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a>, he proclaimed himself a prophet and gave his people a new religion with a scripture in the Berber language. The Barghawata kept this faith for over three centuries until the Almoravid conquest.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Salih ibn Tarif is remembered as the second king of the Berghouata, the prophet of a new Abrahamic religion and the eponymous ancestor of the Oulad Salah tribe of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>. The eleventh-century Andalusian scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-bakri">Al-Bakri</a> reports that he was a Judean (Yahūd) who claimed descent from Simeon, son of Jacob, son of Isaac. According to some sources he regarded himself as a successor to Muhammad, had ten disciples (Ṣahāba) and many wives, and claimed to be able to speak with the dead and heal the sick.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>He is said to have claimed to be the final <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>, with Isa (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>) as his companion who would pray behind him. He proclaimed that his name was Ṣāliḥ in Arabic, Mālik in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a>, Rūbyā in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> and Werba in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a>, and that no prophet would come after him.</p><p>Arabic writers such as Ibn Hazm and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a> describe tenets that set his religion apart from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>: capital punishment for theft, no limit on the number of wives, fasting in the month of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajab">Rajab</a> instead of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramadan">Ramadan</a>, ten obligatory daily prayers instead of five, different ways of performing ablution and prayer, and a ban on marriage between cousins.</p><p>At the age of 47 he left the kingdom for the east, promising to return in the reign of its seventh king. He told his son Ilyās to support the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> and to profess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in public, revealing his own religion only once he was strong enough; it was his grandson Yūnus who did so.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Islamic literature treats his belief as heretical. Its political motive was presumably to secure the Berghouata's independence from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a>, in a manner compared with Kharijism and the earlier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donatism">Donatism</a>, by giving the state an ideology of its own. Some modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> activists regard him as a Berber hero for resisting the Umayyad-Arab conquest and founding the Berghouata state.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Salih ibn Tarif” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their first leader, Tarif, took part in the revolt, and around 744 his son <b>Salih ibn Tarif</b> proclaimed himself a prophet, founding a new religion for his people.</blockquote><a href="/state/barghawata">Barghawata</a></li></ul></section>
